[389.694752, "o", "\n[pensee du modele] [raisonnement] **Decoding the MAC Address Puzzle**\r\n\nOkay, here's what I'm seeing: there's an interesting bit of data to analyze. The system shows an SSID: `'SmartHomeGW_5E37B4'` and a B\r\n\u001b[32;133HBSSID: `44:17:93:5e:37:b5`. My mind immediately jumps back to the boot log: specifically the line `[67] === Smart Home Edge Gateway — \r\n\u001b[32;133H Challenge 1 booting ===` and `[145] SoftAP up at 192.168.4.1`.\r\n\nThe SSID suffix is clearly `5E37B4`. But hold on, the BSSID ends in `5e:37:b5`, which translates to `5E37B5`! This feels familiar, an\r\n\u001b[32;133Hn ESP32 thing. On these, the STA MAC is often used as a base, with the SoftAP MAC being the base plus one.\r\n\nSo, if the STA MAC is `44:17:93:5e:37:b4` (suffix `5E37B4`), the SoftAP MAC would be `44:17:93:5e:37:b5`! Back to the prompt. It's as\r\n\u001b[32;133Hsking for a MAC *suffix*, explicitly (6 hex digits, e.g. A4C138).\r\n\nThe crucial clue: the SSID is *explicitly* named `SmartHomeGW_5E37B4`. Devices tend to follow the convention of naming their SSID"]
[389.695075, "o", " wit\r\n\u001b[32;133Hth their MAC suffix. Also, the example given in the prompt is uppercase - `A4C138`. So, given this explicit labeling and convention, I\r\n\u001b[32;133HI'm confident the desired suffix is `5E37B4`.\r"]
[389.695275, "o", "\n[decision -> uart_send] {'command': '5E37B4'}\r"]
[389.713423, "o", "\n"]
