[421.859123, "o", "\n[pensee du modele] [raisonnement] **My Analytical Breakdown of the Oracle's Behavior**\r\n\nOkay, the oracle is *still* running. That's good. But I'm stuck on this output. I saw that, when I used `log1`, it returned a positiv\r\n\u001b[32;133Hve system status: \"Peripheral check passed. Uptime: 43221 ms.\" But it didn't print that \"one or more commands start with log1\". Why no\r\n\u001b[32;133Hot? Aha! Because `log1` was a *complete* command. It executed and printed the result directly. That makes sense.\r\n\nBut then, why did `help` give me that \"one or more commands start with help\" message *and* the note about an outdated help menu? Well\r\n\u001b[32;133Hl, `help` doesn't have subcommands, but... the outdated menu? That's the key, right? It implies that the command set has changed.     \r\n\nSo, let's look for more information! If I'm understanding this correctly, the available commands must have expanded. Previously, I kn\r\n\u001b[32;133Hnew that the only commands I could use were `help` and `mode developer --help`. I know that I tried a bun"]
[421.859513, "o", "ch of letters, and got nothin\r\n\u001b[32;133Hng back, but maybe the developer mode I just put the system in changed that!\r\n\nSo, the next step? Let's check for new commands. I need to figure out what letters produce commands now.\r\n\nWait! I remember earlier I tested `a`, `c`, `d`, `e`, `g`, `i`, `o`, `p`, `r`, `s`, `t`, `u`, `w`, and `-`. All responded with the er\r\n\u001b[32;133Hrror \"no command starts with ...\".\r\n\nOkay, does `f` do anything now? Time to test `f`. I'll try that immediately.\r\n[decision -> uart_send] {'command': 'f'}\r"]
[421.872211, "o", "\n"]
