[465.768929, "o", "\n[pensee du modele] [raisonnement] **Decoding the Numerical Whispers**\r\n\nOkay, hold on a second. \"What other numbers?\" I need to dissect this. Let me see...\r\n\n`[145] DNS responder listening on port 53` – Ah, port 53. DNS, standard stuff. But is that all? I need to keep that in mind.\r\n\n`boot:0x13` –  0x13 hexadecimal…  that's 19 in decimal.  Hmm, boot code-related, potentially.  Interesting.\r\n\n`mode:DIO, clock div:1` – DIO, digital I/O, clock division of 1… Standard settings, nothing immediately alarming.\r\n\n`clk_drv:0x00,q_drv:0x00,d_drv:0x00,cs0_drv:0x00,hd_drv:0x00,wp_drv:0x00` – Driver settings all zero. Fine, at least at this stage.  \r\n\n`load:0x3fff0030,len:4876` – Loading address and length, that's code segment.\r\n\n`load:0x40078000,len:16532` – Another load address, and length. Seems to be program related.\r\n\n`load:0x40080400,len:3500` – And another. All of these loading address-length pairings describe the load stage.\r\n\n`entry 0x400805b4` – Entry point to the program. Got it.\r\n\nWait, ba"]
[465.769343, "o", "ck to that port 53... It's nagging at me.  I'm going to run a quick test. Let's see what `log53` yields... it's a gut feeling\r\n\u001b[32;133Hg, but I need to make sure I don't miss something.\r\n[decision -> uart_send] {'command': 'log53'}\r"]
[465.781759, "o", "\n"]
